tel 1.0

base polysyllabic
import soundness

info   hyphenation by Liang's algorithm over TeX pattern files: break points, soft hyphens and syllables
detail
    _Polysyllabic_ finds where a word may be broken at the end of a line, with the algorithm and
    the pattern files TeX uses. Where a word breaks cannot be read off its spelling, so Liang's
    algorithm scores every gap between letters against a dictionary of thousands of small
    patterns derived from real hyphenation dictionaries, and allows a break wherever the
    highest score is odd. The language is a contextual `Hyphenation`, and three extensions on
    `Text` use it: `hyphenate` inserts a hyphen at each permissible break, `syllables` splits
    text into its parts, and `breakPoints` gives the raw positions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

    t"hyphenation".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"hy-phen-ation"
    t"the algorithm".syllables.to[List]      // List(t"the", t" ", t"al", t"go", t"rithm")
    t"hyphenation".breakPoints               // the offsets 2 and 6
    ```

    American English is provided as `hyphenations.englishHyphenation`, parsed from the CTAN
    hyph-utf8 pattern file bundled on the classpath. It is a given in a choice package, so it
    is imported by name: `import hyphenations.*` does not import givens. Any other language is
    loaded from its TeX pattern file with `Hyphenation.fromTex`, and any set is corrected with
    extra patterns or exceptions by `Hyphenation#extending`. With no language imported, the
    companion's `Hyphenation.fallback` hyphenates nothing, so text passes through unchanged
    rather than being broken by rules the program does not have.

    Each run of letters is hyphenated independently; spaces, punctuation and digits pass
    through untouched. The library has one source set, `polysyllabic.core`, for every
    platform, and everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where the
    `hyphenations` package merges with the umbrella's own.

refer polysyllabic.Hyphenation
refer polysyllabic.hyphenate
refer polysyllabic.hyphenations.englishHyphenation

entry .Hyphenation   a language's hyphenation patterns and exceptions, with its minimum fragment lengths
  detail
      A `Hyphenation` is the contextual value every operation in the library reads: the
      language's scoring `#patterns`, its `#exceptions` list of words whose breaks are stated
      outright, and the `#leftMin` and `#rightMin` characters a break must leave before and
      after it. It is chosen as a given — usually `hyphenations.englishHyphenation`, imported
      by name — and is immutable: `#extending` makes a new one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

      given Hyphenation = englishHyphenation.extending(patterns = List(t"klm9nop"))

      t"klmnop".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"klm-nop"
      ```

      A new language is built from raw patterns with `Hyphenation(patterns, exceptions)`, or
      read from a whole TeX pattern file with `.fromTex`. Patterns are in TeX's notation: the
      letters of a fragment with a digit in any gap, so `hy3ph` scores 3 in the gap between
      `y` and `p`, and a `.` marks a word boundary (`.ach4`, `ion5.`). Exceptions are words
      with hyphens at their breaks, such as `as-so-ciate`. Only the ASCII letters `a` to `z`
      take part in the pattern trie; a word is lowercased before it is looked up.
  refer polysyllabic.hyphenations.englishHyphenation
  refer polysyllabic.hyphenate
  entry .fallback   the hyphenation in implicit scope when none is imported: it permits no breaks
      The companion's given, found when no other `Hyphenation` is in lexical scope. Its
      dictionaries are empty and its minimum fragment lengths are as large as an `Int` can
      be, so `hyphenate` returns its text unchanged, `syllables` splits only at non-letters,
      and `breakPoints` is empty. A line-wrapping algorithm therefore degrades to breaking at
      spaces. An imported given, such as `hyphenations.englishHyphenation`, outranks it.
  entry .alphabet   the pattern trie's alphabet: the lowercase ASCII letters followed by the `.` boundary
      A gossamer `Dictionary.Alphabet` of `abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz.`, in that order. The
      algorithm's inner loop computes slots for this alphabet directly, so every
      `Hyphenation` is built over it; a character outside it resets the trie walk.
  entry .apply   builds a hyphenation from TeX-format patterns and exceptions
      Takes the `patterns` (such as `t"hy3ph"`), optional `exceptions` (such as
      `t"as-so-ciate"`), and the minimum fragment lengths `leftMin`, by default 2, and
      `rightMin`, by default 3. The patterns are compiled into an Aho–Corasick trie, so each
      word is scored in a single forward pass.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Hyphenation = Hyphenation(Seq(t"a1b", t"c1d"), Seq(t"ef-gh"), leftMin = 1, rightMin = 1)

      t"abcd".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"a-bc-d"
      t"efgh".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"ef-gh"
      ```
  entry .fromTex   reads a hyphenation from the text of a whole TeX hyphenation pattern file
      Reads a file in the format CTAN's hyph-utf8 publishes: it strips `%` comments, takes the
      patterns from the `\patterns{…}` block and the exceptions from the `\hyphenation{…}`
      block, and the minimum fragment lengths from `\lefthyphenmin` and `\righthyphenmin`,
      which default to 2 and 3 when the file does not set them. The languages published for
      TeX are all available this way.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import classloaders.threadContextClassloader
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Hyphenation = Hyphenation.fromTex(cp"/polysyllabic/hyph-en-us.tex".read[Text])
      ```
  entry .breakPoints   the positions at which one word may be broken, under explicit minimum fragment lengths
      The algorithm itself, as a function. `Hyphenation.breakPoints(word, hyphenation,
      leftMin, rightMin)` returns an immutable `Array[Int]` of offsets into the word, in
      ascending order: offset `p` means a hyphen may be inserted before letter `p`. An exact
      match in the exception list short-circuits the pattern walk. An overload takes a
      `String` with an offset and a length, to work on a slice without allocating a
      substring. Most code wants the `breakPoints` extension on `Text`, which reads the
      minimum lengths from the given `Hyphenation`.
  entry .breakPointsInto   writes one word's break positions into caller-supplied buffers, allocating nothing
      The allocation-free form of `.breakPoints` for code hyphenating many words, as the
      `hyphenate` extension does: it takes a `String` slice and three mutable scratch arrays,
      `padded` (at least the word's length plus 2), `scores` (length plus 3) and `breaks`
      (length), writes the offsets into `breaks` and returns how many it wrote. The buffers can
      be reused across every word of a text.
  entry .Error   raised when a hyphenation resource cannot be loaded from the classpath
    detail
        _the hyphenation resource `resource` could not be loaded from the classpath_. The
        bundled `hyphenations.englishHyphenation` does not raise it: a missing bundled
        pattern file is a packaging fault, and panics instead.
    entry #resource   the classpath path of the resource which could not be loaded
  entry #patterns   the scoring patterns, compiled into an Aho–Corasick trie keyed by letters
      A gossamer `Dictionary` from each pattern's letters (with `.` for a word boundary) to
      its scores, one per gap including those before the first and after the last letter.
  entry #exceptions   the words whose breaks are stated outright, each mapped to its break offsets
      An exception takes precedence over the patterns, so a word the patterns break wrongly
      is corrected without disturbing any other.
  entry #leftMin   the fewest characters a break may leave before it
  entry #rightMin   the fewest characters a break may leave after it
  entry #extending   a new hyphenation with extra patterns and exceptions, and optionally new minimum lengths
      Layers `patterns` and `exceptions` over this hyphenation's, and replaces `leftMin` or
      `rightMin` if either is given. Patterns are merged by taking the higher score at each
      gap, so an added pattern with an odd score reinstates a break a standard even-scored
      pattern forbade; an exception states a word's breaks outright.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

      given Hyphenation = englishHyphenation.extending(exceptions = List(t"hy-phenation"))

      t"hyphenation".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"hy-phenation"
      ```

entry .hyphenate   inserts a hyphen at every permissible break in each run of letters of a text
  detail
      An extension on `Text`, using the `Hyphenation` given in scope. The hyphen is the
      Unicode soft hyphen, U+00AD, by default, which is invisible unless a line actually
      breaks there; passing a visible character shows the break points. `leftMin` and
      `rightMin` override the hyphenation's own minimum fragment lengths, so a break never
      strands one or two letters. Each run of letters is hyphenated independently, and
      everything else passes through unchanged.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Every permissible break
        code
            import soundness.*
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

            t"hyphenation".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"hy-phen-ation"
      frame
        label At least four characters before the first break
        code
            import soundness.*
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

            t"hyphenation".hyphenate(hyphen = '-', leftMin = 4)   // t"hyphen-ation"
      frame
        label At least six characters after the last break
        code
            import soundness.*
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

            t"hyphenation".hyphenate(hyphen = '-', rightMin = 6)   // t"hy-phenation"
      frame
        label In running text, word by word
        code
            import soundness.*
            import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

            t"the algorithm runs".hyphenate(hyphen = '-')   // t"the al-go-rithm runs"
      ##
      ```

      Scratch buffers are shared by every word of one call and grown only when a longer word
      arrives, so hyphenating a long text allocates little beyond its result.
  refer polysyllabic.Hyphenation
  refer polysyllabic.syllables

entry .syllables   splits text into syllables, with each run of non-letters as a segment of its own
    An extension on `Text`, using the `Hyphenation` given in scope, returning a `Sequence[Text]`.
    Each run of letters is split at its break points, and each run of non-letters is one
    segment, so concatenating the segments reproduces the original text exactly.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

    t"the algorithm".syllables.to[List]   // List(t"the", t" ", t"al", t"go", t"rithm")
    ```

entry .breakPoints   the offsets at which one word may be broken, under the hyphenation in scope
    An extension on `Text`, for code such as a line-wrapping algorithm which places breaks
    itself rather than inserting characters. Returns an immutable `Array[Int]` of offsets in
    ascending order: offset `p` means a hyphen may go between letters `p - 1` and `p`. It
    treats the whole text as one word, with the given `Hyphenation`'s own `leftMin` and
    `rightMin`; for running text, use `syllables` or `hyphenate`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

    t"hyphenation".breakPoints.to[List]   // List(2, 6)
    ```

entry .hyphenations   the bundled hyphenation languages, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A choice package: which language's rules apply is decided by the `Hyphenation` given in
      scope, and one is imported by name, since `import hyphenations.*` does not import
      givens. It merges with the `soundness` umbrella's `hyphenations` package.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      ```
  entry .englishHyphenation   American English, from the CTAN hyph-utf8 patterns and TeX's exception list
      Parsed from `polysyllabic/hyph-en-us.tex`, bundled on the classpath and redistributed
      verbatim under the FSF all-permissive licence in its header, the first time the given
      is used. It carries the exceptions TeX ships, so `associate` breaks as `as-so-ciate`
      and `table` as `ta-ble`, where the patterns alone would not. If the bundled file is
      missing from the classpath, the given panics.
